## DIRECTION / MONOTONICITY (reported as-is)

* Raw gap_pp: below_median -20.68 pp -> at_or_above_median -1.04 pp (CI includes 0). RR 0.721 -> 0.988.
* The scoping expectation (widest gap at low SES, persisting at high SES) is HALF-CONFIRMED and HALF-REFUTED: widest at low SES = CONFIRMED; persisting at high SES = REFUTED for background SES - the gap closes to ~1 pp (not statistically distinguishable from zero).
* No SES level shows a Black ADVANTAGE in the primary outcome (max RR = 0.99, CI 0.93-1.05).
* Secondary P1 (grade >= 13): BLACK below reference at both levels (RR 0.74 and 0.77); BLACK x SES interaction NOT significant (LR chi2 = 1.11, df = 2, p = 0.573) - the post-secondary gap does not vary detectably with SES.
* Secondary P2 (dorm enrolment proxy) is NON-MONOTONE in the opposite direction: gap -0.53 pp (RR 0.88, ns) below median vs -8.73 pp (RR 0.44) at/above median -> for actual college residence the Black-reference deficit is LARGEST at high SES. Shown unsmoothed; interpretation limited by 91% coverage and coarse residence coding.

## Robustness of the SES definition (primary outcome, gap_pp BLACK vs ref)

| SES definition | gap_pp below-median | gap_pp at/above median |
