| F. family income 1979 alone | -24.11 | -12.98 |
| ENDOGENOUS wealth 1985 (post-outcome) | -13.48 | -12.43 |
| ENDOGENOUS wealth 1996 (post-outcome) | -20.15 | -13.33 |
| ENDOGENOUS income 1985 (post-outcome) | -16.66 | -12.94 |
| ENDOGENOUS income 1989 (post-outcome) | -20.29 | -7.20 |
| ENDOGENOUS savings 1989 (post-outcome) | -18.96 (low) | -20.96 |

Direction (BLACK below reference) is unchanged in every specification. The CLOSING of the gap at high SES holds for all 1979 background measures (A-E) but NOT for the endogenous 1985-1996 measures (F, wealth/income/savings), as expected: conditioning on post-outcome wealth absorbs attainment itself, so those columns are reported only to confirm direction, never to define SES level.

## Missingness handling (no outcome imputation)

* SES item missingness by cohort (%): occupation 34.3 BLACK / 47.5 HISP / 22.4 NBNH; index missing 8.3 / 12.6 / 5.0 -> differential, as anticipated.
* >=2-of-3 rule keeps 11,747 of 12,686 respondents with a usable index (vs 6,888 complete-case).
* Complete-case sensitivity: pattern unchanged (low -21.3 pp, high +1.6 pp).
* Missing-indicator diagnostic: among the 273 primary-outcome respondents WITHOUT a usable index, the Black-reference gap is -16.7 pp (RR 0.73), i.e. same direction as the low-SES group; the missing respondents are NOT a high-SES reservoir, so complete-case-style upward bias of Black SES is not driving the result.
* Primary-outcome restriction itself is an age restriction (73% of the file was <20 in 1979); it is not a missing-data exclusion.

